ny_body='''<section class="page-hero"><div class="hero-wallpaper" aria-hidden="true"><div class="wallpaper-line" style="top:18%">NEW YORK / LONG ISLAND</div><div class="wallpaper-line w2" style="top:58%">FINANCING STRENGTH BEFORE YOU OFFER</div></div><div class="wrap page-hero-inner"><div class="crumbs"><a href="index.html">Home</a> / New York & Long Island</div><span class="eyebrow">Updated September 2026</span><h1>New York & Long Island Mortgage Loans</h1><p class="lead"><strong>Financing strength before you make an offer.</strong> Compare conventional, jumbo, self-employed, bank-statement, asset-based, Non-QM and investor financing before the property creates a deadline.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-blue" href="apply.html">Build a Strong Pre-Approval</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="contact.html">Discuss a New York Scenario</a></div></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><span class="eyebrow">Offer readiness</span><h2>How Can Financing Strength Help Before You Make an Offer on Long Island?</h2><p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> A strong pre-approval can show that the borrower’s income, assets, credit, loan size and intended property type have been reviewed before the offer is submitted. It does not guarantee final approval, but it can reduce avoidable financing uncertainty.</p><div class="grid grid-3"><div class="card"><h3>Purchase Financing</h3><p>Structure the file around the actual purchase price, occupancy, property type, reserves and closing timeline.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Jumbo / High-Value Property</h3><p>High-value New York and Long Island purchases may require a jumbo structure or a comparison with conforming/high-balance financing.</p><a href="jumbo-loans.html">Jumbo financing →</a></div><div class="card"><h3>Self-Employed</h3><p>Traditional income analysis, bank statements and other documentation methods can produce materially different results.</p><a href="self-employed-borrowers.html">Self-employed options →</a></div><div class="card"><h3>Bank Statement / Non-QM</h3><p>Alternative income documentation may be appropriate for business owners and borrowers whose tax returns do not fully reflect cash flow.</p><a href="bank-statement-loans.html">Bank statement →</a> · <a href="non-qm-loans.html">Non-QM →</a></div><div class="card"><h3>Assets as a Qualification Tool</h3><p>Depending on the program, eligible assets may support qualifying income through Agency-permitted income types or lender-specific asset-utilization approaches.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Investment Property / DSCR</h3><p>For rentals, compare conventional rental-income rules with DSCR and other investor programs rather than assuming one method.</p><a href="investment-property-loans.html">Investor loans →</a> · <a href="dscr-loans.html">DSCR →</a></div></div></div></section>
<section class="bg-light"><div class="wrap"><h2>What Mortgage Options Are Available for Self-Employed Buyers in New York?</h2><p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Self-employed borrowers may be able to qualify through traditional tax-return analysis, bank-statement programs, asset-based or asset-utilization methods, and other Non-QM structures. The goal is to compare legitimate qualification methods before assuming the borrower does or does not qualify.</p><h3>Can Assets Be Used to Qualify for a Mortgage?</h3><p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Sometimes. Fannie Mae permits certain employment-related assets, interest/dividend income, retirement income and other documented income types under specific eligibility rules. Separate Non-QM programs may use their own asset-utilization calculations. Assets used for down payment, closing or reserves can affect what remains available for qualification.</p><h3>When Should a New York Buyer Consider a Jumbo Loan?</h3><p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Compare jumbo when the loan amount exceeds applicable conforming limits or when the borrower profile, reserves, high-value property or pricing may make a jumbo execution competitive. The comparison is most useful before an offer is written.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><h2>New York & Long Island Mortgage Questions</h2>'''+''.join(f'<details class="acc"><summary>{q}</summary><div class="acc-body">{a}</div></details>' for q,a in ny_faq)+'''<div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-blue" href="jumbo-loans.html">Jumbo</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="self-employed-borrowers.html">Self-Employed</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="bank-statement-loans.html">Bank Statement</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="non-qm-loans.html">Non-QM</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="dscr-loans.html">DSCR</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="investment-property-loans.html">Investor Loans</a></div></div></section>'''

